day.common:
You are painting one segment of a 360-degree PAINTED HORIZON MATTE (the film matte-painting trick) for Pine Hollow, a PHOTOREAL first-person game set in the northern boreal forest (taiga): dark spruce and Scots pine forest with patches of birch, granite crags and a mountain range to the north. It must look like a real photograph taken from a fire-lookout tower (like the view from a North American or Scandinavian fire lookout), not a painting, not concept art.

The attached image is a real in-game frame of the EMPTY SKY seen from the deck of the fire-lookout tower, which stands on a ridge about 60 m above the surrounding forest canopy. The camera pitch is exactly level, 95 degree horizontal field of view, 72 degree vertical field of view. The eye-level horizon is EXACTLY on the horizontal middle of the image (y = 50 %). EDIT that frame: keep the exact framing, keep the sky gradient of the top half as it is, and paint the FAR boreal country seen from the lookout:
- ABOVE the middle line: the far skyline 10-60 km away: rolling forested ridges and low mountains, their dark conifer forest turned blue-grey and soft by aerial perspective; the far skyline sits 2-6 % of the image height above the middle line (the land's top edge must be CRISP against the sky, clearly readable as distant hills); nothing is taller than 14 % of the image height above the middle line;
- BELOW the middle line (the lower half): the country seen from above: layer after layer of forested ridges and hills receding into the distance, each farther layer paler and bluer (strong aerial perspective), thin pale valley haze lying between the ridges; the nearest layer, at the bottom 15 % of the image, is dark green conifer-forested hills 2-4 km away where individual spruce crowns are just readable, with its lowest part dissolving into soft pale valley haze at the very bottom edge;
- photoreal natural colours of a clear midday in the northern summer: deep green-black spruce, some lighter green birch and grey granite outcrops on the nearer hills, blue haze in the distance; a few flat white fair-weather cloud shadows are fine;
- nothing near the camera: no tower, no railing, no foreground trees, no people, no animals, no birds, no roads, no clearings with buildings, no power lines, no man-made anything, no text, no UI, no watermark, no frame, no vignette;
- the upper 40 % of the image stays the plain clean sky gradient from the reference (no clouds, no sun, no birds): it will be cut away;
- the LEFT 25 % and the RIGHT 25 % of the image hold only the continuous low forested ridgelines (skyline under 4 % of the image height above the middle line, no peaks, no lakes) so that the neighbouring segments of the panorama join; the special features go in the middle 50 %.

